WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.044 --> 00:00:03.680
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.880
- Welcome to Ear Biscuits, I'm Rhett.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.250
- And I'm Link.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.540
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.900
we're asking the question
if you were a boat,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.770
what type of boat would you be?

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.880
- And this might strike
you as an odd question,

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.420
maybe you've thought about this before.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.110
We actually got--
- I've never thought about it.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:27.100
- We got this question
from Mr. Alex Punch,

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.590
who has moved onto
bigger and better things.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.090
Still remains good friends,
he actually stopped by today.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.730
He didn't tell me he was gonna be here.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:39.800
- Mythical crew member emeritus.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.900
- Yeah, so--
- Let's call him that so

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.230
we're happy for Alex as
he had a great opportunity

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.960
working for somebody. (scoffs)

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.490
It's not me so I, once he
told me he had an opportunity

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.500
and we heard about the
specifics, I was like,

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.970
oh yeah, okay man.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.050
I don't remember.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:57.790
I'm not gonna promote his company.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.480
- Well we wish him well, we wish him well.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.700
- We wish him well, we love him.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.780
- And we're using one of
his ideas for this podcast.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.268
He actually, this is an idea
that he actually pitched

00:01:06.268 --> 00:01:07.453
for an Ear Biscuit.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:13.493
He said that his mother
was a child therapist.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.710
- He said counselor I
think, maybe therapist too.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.360
- Yeah, something along those lines.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.000
And this is a question
that she gets the children

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.310
that she works with to ask and answer.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.090
- And if we've proven anything,

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.930
if something's good enough for children,

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.280
it's good enough for us.
- For man children?

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.830
- Yeah.
- Good enough for children,

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:33.910
good enough for man children.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.670
- So really this was, I
mean it was basically,

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.070
it was an idea conceived by Alex's mom,

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:41.827
along with Alex conceived
by his mom by the way.

00:01:41.827 --> 00:01:44.654
- It's probably in some
sort of book or something

00:01:44.654 --> 00:01:46.285
that she studied.
- Well.

00:01:46.285 --> 00:01:47.118
- You think she came up with it?

00:01:47.118 --> 00:01:48.010
Let's credit it.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:49.880
- Let's just say we came up with it.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.350
Let's cut everything we've just said.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:52.597
- Okay all right, we came
up with this question.

00:01:52.597 --> 00:01:54.460
- We got this idea that
we think will be good

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.780
for child counselors and therapists,

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.730
what kind of boat would
you be if you could be

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:03.730
but because it gives insight
into how you view your own--

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.590
- Self.
- Self, your personhood,

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:07.423
your personality.

00:02:07.423 --> 00:02:09.261
- And we're gonna answer
the question for each other.

00:02:09.261 --> 00:02:10.260
- We'll get into that.
- We're gonna answer

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.430
the question for each other as well.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.122
We're gonna guess which boat,

00:02:14.122 --> 00:02:16.260
I'm not trying to guess which boat

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:17.580
you described yourself as,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.450
I'm just describing, my thought
was to describe what boat

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.480
I thought you were and
you may or may not agree.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.530
Then you can tell me what
boat you are and vice versa.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.210
- I know what boat I think I am

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.520
and I know what boat I think you are

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.320
which I incidentally think is the boat

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:32.680
that you think you are.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.230
- And I would be, but we are gonna talk

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:36.063
about the different types of boats

00:02:36.063 --> 00:02:38.630
and so my answers may change,
but as it stands right now,

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:41.520
just with my limited knowledge
of boats coming into this,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.260
I'm not a boat master.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.290
I like to think of myself as a waterman,

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.973
but I've only been in a few boats.

00:02:47.973 --> 00:02:49.310
- What is a waterman?

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.130
- It's just a man of the water.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:52.610
- And you think of yourself that way?

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.060
You think of yourself
as a man of the water?

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.465
- Yeah like my heritage, you know, those--

00:02:57.465 --> 00:02:58.298
- Oh.

00:02:58.298 --> 00:03:01.650
- The Scots up on the lochs.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.450
You know, crossing the lochs
and swimming and boating

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.950
and Vikings, pillaging villages.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.245
- I get it.

00:03:08.245 --> 00:03:10.103
- But not really doing that personally

00:03:10.103 --> 00:03:13.630
'cause I get a little seasick, you know.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.884
- I think of myself as a soilman.

00:03:15.884 --> 00:03:17.420
- Yeah.
- Like a man of the soil.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:18.570
- And I think that your last name,

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:19.840
we've talked about this at some point,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.110
I think that your name
Neal means to like kneel

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.750
and plant a seed. (chuckles)

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:27.033
- It doesn't.

00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:29.047
- A man who kneels.
- I don't know what it means

00:03:29.047 --> 00:03:31.911
but that ain't it.
- And caresses the soil.

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.760
I think it's a farming thing,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.420
I do think it is a farming thing.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.253
- Yeah.

00:03:36.253 --> 00:03:38.050
- You know a man of the
soil, a man of the water.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.950
Water's gotta have soil,
it's always gotta have water,

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.280
so you have mud.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.880
That's why we're the muddy boys.

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:48.072
- We're the muddied boys, okay so we're--

00:03:48.072 --> 00:03:49.910
- No not muddied boys,
it's the muddy boys.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.410
- But we should talk about yesterday

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:55.290
'cause yesterday was
technically a work day, yet--

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.530
- Speaking of child therapy.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.323
- We didn't, took advantage of,

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.250
took advantage of time that we rarely have

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.374
to not come into the
office and also red alert,

00:04:07.374 --> 00:04:09.463
not really work that much.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.540
Last, like coming back
from Thanksgiving break,

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.110
I looked at the whole
calendar year for 2019

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:20.620
and I was like, I wanna go
ahead and put all the stuff

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.030
on the calendar that we know about,

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:23.880
like we're doing a lot of touring stuff,

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.460
we're shooting a lot of GMM of course.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:32.130
I wanna know every day in 2019
that we're shooting anything

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.447
and obligated to stuff and
I wanna put blackout dates,

00:04:35.447 --> 00:04:39.370
like the credit card
companies do or Disney does

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:40.933
when you buy an annual pass.

00:04:42.107 --> 00:04:44.230
- Right.
- So Jenna and I

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.190
worked very hard on this calendar.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.653
- Oh so that blacked out
Monday was months old?

00:04:50.653 --> 00:04:52.680
- That's Thanksgiving of last year, yeah.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.470
- Oh wow.
- I guess I didn't tell you.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.490
- You didn't tell me
'cause it was Sunday night,

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.260
my wife was with you and Christy--

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:59.093
- I was hanging out
with, yeah, with Jessie,

00:04:59.093 --> 00:04:59.926
she was hanging out with us.
- She said,

00:04:59.926 --> 00:05:01.179
"Are you working tomorrow?"

00:05:01.179 --> 00:05:02.040
I was like, "Yeah."

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.586
And she's like, "Well Link's not."

00:05:03.586 --> 00:05:05.716
- And she was with me and she was like,

00:05:05.716 --> 00:05:07.720
I was like listen, please don't--

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:08.553
- You gotta tell me these things.

00:05:08.553 --> 00:05:09.690
- Don't put me in the middle of this.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:11.853
It's on the calendar.
- Yeah but it's--

00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:14.260
- It's on the special
Link and Jenna calendar--

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.870
- But it's a Monday--
- That you were invited to.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.609
- It's a Monday though, see I mean--

00:05:18.609 --> 00:05:21.150
- Yeah but after tour, I'm, anyway--

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.080
- No I'm glad, I'm glad I didn't come in.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.750
- 'Cause watch out
buying that annual pass.

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:27.490
Look at the blackout dates first.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:28.457
- You show up to Disney and you're like--

00:05:28.457 --> 00:05:30.380
- And they turn you away.
- What, no.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:31.310
- You have to pay, you have

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:34.183
to drop 1,000 quid to get in there.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.030
- So we had a day with our,

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.903
I mean I had a day with
Shepherd, my youngest.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.670
You had a day with--
- Well me and Christy hung out

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:44.900
and then--
- Your middle.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.130
- Well I mean Christy
and I were gonna hang out

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:52.130
and do some stuff and the
mail had really piled up,

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:57.560
but then Lily was sick.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:00.060
She called and had to
come home from school.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.020
I was like well, now you gotta go get her

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.670
and now I gotta figure
this out and I gotta,

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:07.013
I'm gonna do a little bit of work.

00:06:07.013 --> 00:06:10.670
But then I picked up Lando
and then I picked up Lincoln

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.890
from school, it's just like
whoa, that just feels great

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:14.720
to pick up a kid from school,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.440
feels like I'm dadding so hard.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.570
Look at me drivin' up,
get in the car, kid.

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.616
I'm gonna be bangin' some Chicago.

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:25.210
Blastin' Chicago.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.650
I'm not bangin' Chicago.

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.837
Sounds like I'm having
sex with the band Chicago.

00:06:30.837 --> 00:06:32.620
- Yeah.
- Which I've never been given

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.890
that opportunity so I
can't say what I would do.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.640
- Or the city of Chicago.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:39.535
I'm sure there's a place,
there's an area you could find.

00:06:39.535 --> 00:06:41.610
(laughs)

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:42.867
- Blasting (chuckles).

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.692
- Did you blast it for real?
- I blasted it, yes.

00:06:45.692 --> 00:06:47.860
- So with the windows rolled down?

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.050
So you were trying to embarrass your kids?

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.100
- Not with the windows rolled down

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.790
but when he opened the door to
get in, it just spilled out,

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:55.450
Chicago just blew out like the windy city.

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.360
- And what, was there a--
(Link blows air)

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:59.193
Dad, Dad.

00:06:59.193 --> 00:07:02.380
A quick shut of the door, was
there an embarrassment factor?

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:04.348
- Lincoln doesn't do that,
Lily's the one to do that

00:07:04.348 --> 00:07:07.673
and Lando for totally different reasons.

00:07:09.174 --> 00:07:11.030
I won't get into that but
they both have their reasons

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.020
but the middle child, he
just goes with the flow, man,

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.570
whatevs.
- Mm.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:18.730
- And he gets in the car and he's like,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.660
oh you got the bikes on the back.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.850
I'm using your bike rack
a lot, thank you for that.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:23.870
- Yeah you haven't brought it back.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:25.550
- I keep using it.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:26.850
Somebody's gotta use it.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.330
- Well I would--
- I bought Lincoln

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:29.730
a mountain bike--
- Like to use it.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.385
- Bought Link a, well just call me.

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:34.150
- (sputters) That's not how it works.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:35.230
- Just call me if you need it.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.570
I really need it.
- Bring it back to my house

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.250
and then, or get your own.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.931
- Well, I already have yours.

00:07:40.931 --> 00:07:42.430
I really didn't wanna talk about this

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.150
'cause I didn't want it
to come to this but--

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.300
- 'Cause you knew that it would.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.520
- It's mine now.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.139
- No it's not.
- That bike rack's mine.

00:07:48.139 --> 00:07:50.077
It's, you made a good choice in buying it

00:07:50.077 --> 00:07:51.910
'cause it works great--
- Yeah it's a good bike rack.

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:54.000
- So I like how it works.
- It's the best.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:54.833
Highest rated.

00:07:54.833 --> 00:07:57.610
- You should get another
one for your house.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:00.860
- And then I'll sell it to you
for the price of a new one.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:02.070
- I already have one.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.250
- Yeah, you're gonna buy me a new one.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:07.360
- So I actually bought myself a new bike

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.823
and I gave Lincoln my old bike
'cause I got a bigger one.

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:12.943
And--

00:08:12.943 --> 00:08:15.270
- You got a new mountain bike?
- I did, yeah.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.270
- When did this happen?

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:17.313
- I got a 29er.

00:08:18.691 --> 00:08:23.370
When I borrowed the thing
from you like two months ago.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.090
- You got shocks in the
front and back, dual?

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:28.760
- No, I only got 'em in the
front because my old bike,

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:31.530
which Lincoln was going
to ride is a hard tail

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:33.260
and I didn't want him looking at my bike

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:36.230
and have any excuse for why
he doesn't wanna ride with me.

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:38.460
- Oh, okay.
- So mine's a hard tail.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.080
And so he's like oh you
got the bikes in the back?

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.100
I was like yeah remember I told
you I was gonna pick you up

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.096
from school, we're gonna go biking.

00:08:44.096 --> 00:08:47.470
He knows, I can't tell
if Lincoln's into it yet

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:50.510
or if he knows that I'm
so into it that he's just

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.463
being very polite so he
doesn't put up a fight.

00:08:55.271 --> 00:09:00.271
So we went and we were
bikin' on this single track

00:09:03.036 --> 00:09:07.650
and it's, I picked something
that wasn't too ambitious.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:08.870
'Cause I don't wanna burn him.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.990
The thing is, the last
couple times we've been out

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:14.220
I've taken Lando too and
I was very delinquent

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.300
in teaching Lando to ride
a bike, like literally,

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.660
I taught him to ride a
bike three months ago.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:20.820
He's nine years old.
- You're not gonna go

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.860
up and down any hills with that situation.

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:24.570
- But then that bike was
too small so then I had

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.392
to buy him a bike and
the only place in town

00:09:27.392 --> 00:09:29.557
had these, they were like mountain bikes

00:09:29.557 --> 00:09:32.140
and I was like, so he went
from learning to ride a bike

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:36.720
to a week later riding a mountain bike.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.850
But he, that's the key.

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.860
Don't try to teach, parents out there,

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:45.870
don't try to teach your kids
at a young age to do anything.

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:49.160
Wait until they can basically
figure it out on their own

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:50.340
and then take all the credit.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:51.830
- That's not good advice.
- My son learned how

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:55.670
to ride a bike in 10
minutes and two weeks later,

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:59.600
he was on a mountain bike
in the woods because of me.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.860
Waiting until he was way too
old to learn how to do it.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:06.280
That goes for reading, that goes for math,

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:07.360
that goes for--

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.590
- This is questionable on many levels.

00:10:09.590 --> 00:10:11.673
- It goes for like relationships.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.710
Don't have open
communication with your kids

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.650
about anything until
it's basically too late

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:21.453
and then you can take all
the credit for how they're--

00:10:21.453 --> 00:10:24.070
- Screwing them up?
- Doing everything perfectly.

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:25.450
Yeah this is kinda fizzling out.

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.150
- Yeah.
- I'm a soil man, it's,

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:32.783
you know, it's different
for me than most people.

00:10:33.812 --> 00:10:36.900
But I will say we get
out on the single track

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.600
and I was like Lincoln,
you go in front of me

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.520
