00:18:28.641 --> 00:18:31.830
But a Fibonacci sequence,
it's this fascinating thing.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:33.150
In fact I actually think,

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:35.090
I actually ordered a book all about it

00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:37.318
because I'm so fascinated by it

00:18:37.318 --> 00:18:39.524
and I'd heard about it, people
talk about the golden ratio

00:18:39.524 --> 00:18:41.760
but I was always like I don't
really know what that is

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.560
and I'm not gonna look it up.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:45.960
But essentially Fibonacci
sequence is staring

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.490
from the number zero and
one and then continuing

00:18:48.490 --> 00:18:50.280
in a sequence where the
next number is the sum

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:53.480
of the previous two numbers,
so you got zero and then one

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.410
and the next number is
zero plus one which is one,

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.370
the next number is one
plus one which is two.

00:18:57.370 --> 00:18:58.870
And then two plus one which is three,

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:00.780
and then five and then eight and then 13

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:03.671
and then 21 and it continues
to add up like that.

00:19:03.671 --> 00:19:08.671
And then, if you take, the
way you get that pattern

00:19:08.958 --> 00:19:13.610
is if you take squares
that the length of the side

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:16.554
of the squares is the number
in the Fibonacci sequence

00:19:16.554 --> 00:19:20.410
and you continue to connect
the corners of the squares

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:23.130
with the next biggest
one, it goes out like this

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.444
and it creates like a snail pattern.

00:19:25.444 --> 00:19:26.277
- A spiral.

00:19:26.277 --> 00:19:28.819
- A spiral that's very
recognizable pattern.

00:19:28.819 --> 00:19:30.628
But then if you take any two numbers

00:19:30.628 --> 00:19:32.340
in the Fibonacci sequence, the number

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:34.280
and then the number before it

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:37.240
and you divide the number before
it by the number after it,

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.600
you get this number called Phi which

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.270
we know Pi is a very
popular mathematical number

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:45.920
but Phi is like 1.618 something

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:47.460
and that's the--
- A constant.

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:50.490
- That's the golden ratio
and you see it all the time

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:53.850
in nature but also there's
many people who make sculptures

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.940
who were like, I want the
proportions of someone's face,

00:19:56.940 --> 00:19:59.629
the proportions of the length
and the width of their face

00:19:59.629 --> 00:20:03.820
to be Phi and you see it in,

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:06.230
Hank Green's got an old side
show video where he kinda,

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:08.090
in like three minutes kind of explains it

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.697
and talks about the patterns
and how you see these numbers

00:20:10.697 --> 00:20:13.350
and the sequence show up
in nature all the time,

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:17.973
like flowers are usually
have petals of you know,

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:21.910
three, five, eight, 13, 21 and it shows up

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:25.020
in all these places in
nature and it seems like

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:27.659
some magic thing but really it's just,

00:20:27.659 --> 00:20:30.630
it's the most efficient way to pack things

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:33.400
into different pattern,
anyway, super exciting.

